WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.667
The first record expresses an intention. It does not yet prove that anything was sent.

2
00:00:07.667 --> 00:00:14.667
The journal adds the actual attempt with a stable identity: operation T7.

3
00:00:14.667 --> 00:00:23.350
Remote confirmation links T7 to R42 and is recorded separately. An attempt is not a substitute for an outcome.

4
00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:30.850
Compaction reduces the history visible to the model.

5
00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:37.850
The workspace, revision and processes may have changed outside that summary.

6
00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:46.533
Read the operational state again before continuing. Task memory and reality are different objects.

7
00:00:47.033 --> 00:00:54.033
Publication is sent, but its response is lost. The outcome is unknown.

8
00:00:54.033 --> 00:01:01.033
Before retrying, look up the operation using its stable identity.

9
00:01:01.033 --> 00:01:08.033
If it exists, reconcile the receipt. A blind retry could create a duplicate.

10
00:01:08.533 --> 00:01:15.533
Two independent pieces can be built at the same time.

11
00:01:15.533 --> 00:01:22.700
In this example they take four and six minutes, followed by two minutes of integration.

12
00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:29.867
The path takes eight minutes, not six. Gains depend on dependencies, coordination and final tests.

13
00:01:30.367 --> 00:01:37.367
The candidate was verified against base A.

14
00:01:37.367 --> 00:01:44.367
The target branch advances to B while the agent works.

15
00:01:44.367 --> 00:01:51.917
Integration changes the combined state. Review the effective diff and rerun the affected checks.

16
00:01:52.417 --> 00:01:59.417
Delivery identifies the task, final revision and diff of the combined state.

17
00:01:59.417 --> 00:02:07.333
Checks identify their scope and the revision they verified. They are not inherited from an old base.

18
00:02:07.333 --> 00:02:15.250
Delivery includes limits and pending decisions. The reviewer knows what is verified and what is still missing.

